[{:name "identify-an-L1-kernel-from-its-action-on-smooth-test-derivatives"
  :lane :solve-lane
  :level :strategy
  :status :draft
  :body
  {:problem-class
   "Identify an integrable kernel `g` from the identity `g ⋆ f' = f(x+h) - f(x-h)` for every smooth compactly supported test function."
   :standard-results
   ["fundamental theorem of calculus"
    "differentiation of convolution"
    "derivative-zero implies constancy"
    "fundamental lemma of the calculus of variations / distributional uniqueness"]
   :route
   ["Let `χ` be the indicator of `[-h,h]` and set `k = g - χ`."
    "Use the fundamental theorem of calculus to prove `χ ⋆ f' = f(x+h) - f(x-h)`, hence `k ⋆ f' = 0` for every smooth compactly supported `f`."
    "Given an arbitrary test function `q`, reflect it by defining `f(x) = q(-x)`."
    "Use convolution smoothing to obtain `(k ⋆ f)' = k ⋆ f' = 0`, then apply derivative-zero constancy."
    "Prove the constant is zero: `k ⋆ f` is integrable, while a nonzero constant is not integrable over the infinite-volume space `ℝ`."
    "Evaluate the zero convolution at the origin to obtain `∫ q x • k x = 0`."
    "Apply `ae_eq_zero_of_integral_contDiff_smul_eq_zero` and unfold `k` and `χ` to conclude `g = χ` almost everywhere."]
   :infinite-volume-step
   {:role
    "This converts derivative-zero constancy into vanishing, rather than merely identifying an unknown additive constant."
    :api
    ["integrable_const_iff"
     "not_isFiniteMeasure_iff"
     "Real.volume_univ"]}
   :key-api
   ["intervalIntegral.integral_deriv_of_contDiffOn_uIcc"
    "HasCompactSupport.hasDerivAt_convolution_right"
    "Integrable.integrable_convolution"
    "is_const_of_deriv_eq_zero"
    "ae_eq_zero_of_integral_contDiff_smul_eq_zero"]
   :witness
   {:declaration "convolution_identity_implies_indicator"
    :statement-integrity :intact
    :axioms ["propext" "Classical.choice" "Quot.sound"]
    :sorryAx false}
   :rule
   "To identify an `L¹` convolution kernel from its action on test derivatives, subtract the candidate kernel, smooth by convolution, kill the resulting constant using infinite volume, and invoke distributional uniqueness."
